{"kind":"request","text":"Check fixed-slot indexes against a direct scan through storage lifecycles\n\nScope: section 4 of report 7f7def61 (attachments/testing-strategy.md). Three repairs came from the derived fixed-slot index disagreeing with the items it indexes: e7a70e9c (genesis initialization), fe9fefb9 (dependency reads ordered by primary key) and 73528f2d (readiness bound to the actual head after an older writer advanced items without updating the index). The current source is packages/scope/src/sqlite.ts around lines 180 and 232. Strengthen the existing rollforward witness packages/scope/test/fixed-index.test.ts rather than replace it.\n\nUse a small test-side item array and an independent equality predicate as the count oracle. Generate create, transition, repeated absolute row write, transaction rollback, restart, clear and rebuild, and legacy append commands. After each committed command, query present, absent, optional and final-state values. Counts must equal a direct scan. Rebuild keeps the semantic checkpoint. Rollback keeps both items and answers. A marker for an old head cannot establish current readiness. Do not compute expectations with fixedRowsOf, fixedValueOf, fixedCount or MemoryState's index. Start with strings, booleans and missing values.\n\nDistinguishing history: establish readiness, append through the base writer without fixed writes, restart, then query the appended duplicate. Removing the marker and head comparison must fail that history on its count.\n\nBudget: 12 histories of 12 to 20 commands with small item sets. Keep the existing 34,000-row witness separate. Verify with npx vitest run --project derive fixed-slot-index and npx vitest run --project scope fixed-index.\n\nOrder: after 1c7dddfb. The O4 live runs take precedence whenever the browser connection returns.","body":{"conditions":"Reviewed head lands on main with the scan oracle histories and the head-binding control failing by assertion when removed.","target_head":"c44e311c